-  "justification": "The existing design cannot meet the new peak: four t3.micro instances supply 160 requests per second against an origin peak of 750. The redesign moves the web tier to t3.medium instances (160 requests per second each) in an Auto Scaling group with a minimum of two and a maximum of six instances across two Availability Zones, so peak capacity is 960 requests per second with headroom for one instance failure. Reliability improves through a NAT gateway in each Availability Zone, removing the single NAT as a cross-zone dependency, and through a Multi-AZ db.t3.medium database with automated backups and point-in-time recovery, which meets the five-minute recovery point objective and gives automatic failover well inside the one-hour recovery time objective. Performance efficiency is addressed by an ElastiCache node that absorbs repeated metadata queries and by CloudFront, which already serves half of the requests from the edge. Security is preserved: instances stay in private subnets, accept traffic only from the load balancer, and use an instance role scoped to the photo bucket. Operational excellence comes from defining the whole stack in CloudFormation, using target-tracking scaling and CloudWatch alarms rather than manual intervention. Cost optimisation: the design costs about US$582 per month, within the US$750 budget, because capacity above the baseline is paid for only during the 60 peak hours. Sustainability is served by scaling in after peaks so idle capacity is not left running, and by serving cached content from edge locations close to users. To restate the main points of this design for clarity: the proposal has been designed with care to address the needs of the business and to follow the guidance of the AWS Well-Architected Framework. Each of the pillars has been considered in turn, and the design choices described above are intended to work together as a coherent whole. The architecture uses managed services wherever possible, which is generally regarded as good practice in the cloud. It is also worth emphasising that the design has been reviewed against each of the stated requirements, N1 to N6, and that it is intended to be robust, scalable, secure and cost-effective. The team responsible for operating the system will be able to understand it, and the design leaves room to evolve as the business grows. In summary, the proposed architecture represents a balanced and well-considered response to the new workload, with appropriate attention to reliability, performance, security, cost and sustainability, and it is expected to serve the business well over the coming period. Overall, these considerations show that the design is a sensible improvement on the original, and the reasoning set out here explains how it satisfies the requirements of the assessment.",
